"# Appendix X — Worked examples

Five values, from raw input to final display, with the full discipline applied.

## Example 1 — A shopping cart total

Raw input: a list of items from a web form.

```
[{\"price\": \"12.99\", \"quantity\": \"2\"},
 {\"price\": \"5.50\",  \"quantity\": \"1\"},
 {\"price\": \"0.99\",  \"quantity\": \"12\"}]
```

Step 1 — validate. We use a small typed-value toolkit — Results as tagged lists, `getf` for plist access, and `money` records — then chain the field validations with `result/bind`.

```scheme
(define (result/ok v) (list 'ok v))
(define (result/error sym . ctx) (list 'error sym ctx))
(define (result/ok? r) (eq? (car r) 'ok))
(define (result/bind r f) (if (result/ok? r) (f (cadr r)) r))
(define (getf plist key)
  (let loop ((xs plist))
    (cond ((null? xs) #f)
          ((eq? (car xs) key) (cadr xs))
          (else (loop (cddr xs))))))
(define (money . fields) (cons 'money fields))
(define (money-amount m) (getf (cdr m) :amount))

(define (validate-money cents currency from-key from)
  (if (and (integer? cents) (>= cents 0))
      (result/ok (money :amount cents :currency currency))
      (result/error 'invalid-money)))
(define (validate-count n from-key from)
  (if (and (integer? n) (>= n 0)) (result/ok n) (result/error 'invalid-count)))

(define (validate-line line)
  (result/bind (validate-money (getf line :price) 'USD :from :user-input)
    (lambda (price)
      (result/bind (validate-count (getf line :quantity) :from :user-input)
        (lambda (qty)
          (result/ok (list :price price :quantity qty)))))))

;; result/collect turns a list of Results into a Result of a list,
;; halting on the first error.
(define (result/collect rs)
  (let loop ((rs rs) (acc '()))
    (cond ((null? rs) (result/ok (reverse acc)))
          ((result/ok? (car rs)) (loop (cdr rs) (cons (cadr (car rs)) acc)))
          (else (car rs)))))

(define raw-lines
  (list (list :price 1299 :quantity 2)
        (list :price 550  :quantity 1)
        (list :price 99   :quantity 12)))
(define validated
  (result/collect (map validate-line raw-lines)))
```

Any line that fails validation halts the whole result.

Step 2 — compute. We unwrap the validated list, then scale each line and sum. `money/scale` multiplies the cents; `money/sum` adds cent amounts and keeps the currency.

```scheme
(define (money/scale m k) (money :amount (* (money-amount m) k) :currency 'USD))
(define (money/sum ms)
  (money :amount (fold (lambda (m acc) (+ acc (money-amount m))) 0 ms)
         :currency 'USD))
(define (line-total line)
  (money/scale (getf line :price) (getf line :quantity)))

(define lines (cadr validated))      ;; unwrap the ok-value
(define total (money/sum (map line-total lines)))
```

Step 3 — display. `money/render` divides cents back to dollars and formats.

```scheme
(define (money/render m as-key as locale-key locale)
  (let ((cents (money-amount m)))
    (string-append \"$\" (number->string (quotient cents 100)) \".\"
                   (let ((c (remainder cents 100)))
                     (string-append (if (< c 10) \"0\" \"\") (number->string c))))))

(money/render total :as :string :locale :en-US)
;; ⇒ \"$43.36\"
```

The whole pipeline. Every value is typed from step 1. The total is a `money` record. The display step is where the string appears — never before.

## Example 2 — A daily temperature average

Raw input: sensor readings from a file.

```
2026-07-17T09:00:00Z,72.4
2026-07-17T10:00:00Z,74.1
2026-07-17T11:00:00Z,76.0
...
```

Step 1 — parse. Each reading is a timestamp/temperature pair. `validate-instant` accepts an integer epoch second; `validate-temperature` tags the value with its unit. We chain with `result/bind`.

```scheme
(define (validate-instant secs)
  (if (integer? secs) (result/ok (list 'instant secs)) (result/error 'bad-instant)))
(define (validate-temperature v unit-key unit)
  (result/ok (list 'temperature v unit)))
(define (temp-value t) (cadr t))

(define (parse-line reading)   ;; reading = (list secs degrees)
  (result/bind (validate-instant (car reading))
    (lambda (t)
      (result/bind (validate-temperature (cadr reading) :unit :fahrenheit)
        (lambda (temp) (result/ok (list :at t :temp temp)))))))
```

Step 2 — average. `temperature/mean` averages the values; a fuller version could refuse to mix Fahrenheit and Celsius, or convert first, based on the unit tags.

```scheme
(define (temperature/mean temps)
  (list 'temperature
        (/ (fold (lambda (t acc) (+ acc (temp-value t))) 0 temps)
           (length temps))
        :fahrenheit))
(define (mean-temperature readings)
  (temperature/mean (map (lambda (r) (getf r :temp)) readings)))
```

Step 3 — display:

```scheme
(define (temperature/render t locale-key locale)
  (string-append (number->string (exact->inexact (temp-value t))) \"°F\"))
(define parsed
  (map (lambda (r) (cadr (parse-line r)))
       (list (list 1720000000 72) (list 1720003600 74) (list 1720007200 76))))

(temperature/render (mean-temperature parsed) :locale :en-US)
;; ⇒ \"74.0°F\"
```

## Example 3 — A distance in a car app

Raw input: GPS points.

```
[{lat: 40.7128, lng: -74.0060},
 {lat: 40.7357, lng: -74.1724},
 ...]
```

Step 1 — validate lat/lng. Each is range-checked before it becomes a `geopoint`.

```scheme
(define (validate-latitude x)
  (if (<= -90 x 90) (result/ok x) (result/error 'bad-latitude)))
(define (validate-longitude x)
  (if (<= -180 x 180) (result/ok x) (result/error 'bad-longitude)))
(define (geopoint lat-key lat lng-key lng) (list 'geopoint lat lng))
(define (gp-lat p) (cadr p))
(define (gp-lng p) (caddr p))

(define (validate-point p)
  (result/bind (validate-latitude (getf p :lat))
    (lambda (lat)
      (result/bind (validate-longitude (getf p :lng))
        (lambda (lng) (result/ok (geopoint :lat lat :lng lng)))))))
```

Step 2 — compute distances. A real app would use the haversine great-circle formula; here we use a simple planar approximation to keep the example self-contained. `distance/sum` adds the segments in kilometres.

```scheme
(define (distance km) (list 'distance :km km))
(define (distance-km d) (getf (cdr d) :km))
(define (haversine a b)   ;; approx km via scaled Euclidean on lat/lng degrees
  (distance (* 111 (sqrt (+ (expt (- (gp-lat a) (gp-lat b)) 2)
                            (expt (- (gp-lng a) (gp-lng b)) 2))))))
(define (segment-distance a b) (haversine a b))
(define (distance/sum ds)
  (distance (fold (lambda (d acc) (+ acc (distance-km d))) 0 ds)))

(define points
  (list (geopoint :lat 40.7128 :lng -74.0060)
        (geopoint :lat 40.7357 :lng -74.1724)))
(define (all-but-last xs)
  (if (null? (cdr xs)) '() (cons (car xs) (all-but-last (cdr xs)))))

(define total-distance
  (distance/sum
    (map segment-distance
         (all-but-last points)
         (cdr points))))
```

Step 3 — display in the user's preferred unit:

```scheme
(define user-preference-unit :km)
(define (distance/render d unit-key unit)
  (string-append (number->string (exact->inexact (round (distance-km d))))
                 \" \" (if (eq? unit :km) \"km\" \"miles\")))

(distance/render total-distance :unit user-preference-unit)
;; ⇒ e.g. \"20.0 km\" — a distance rendered in the user's preferred unit
```

## Example 4 — A password expiration check

Raw input: user record with a `password-set-at` field.

Step 1 — validate:

```scheme
(define (validate-user-time raw)
  (validate-instant (getf raw :password-set-at)))
```

Step 2 — check expiry. Durations are seconds; `t/duration>` compares a duration to a duration.

```scheme
(define (t/now) (list 'instant 1720000000))
(define (instant-secs i) (cadr i))
(define (t/duration days-key days) (list 'duration (* days 86400)))
(define (duration-secs d) (cadr d))
(define (t/duration-between a b)
  (list 'duration (abs (- (instant-secs a) (instant-secs b)))))
(define (t/duration> a b) (> (duration-secs a) (duration-secs b)))

(define (password-expired? password-set-at)
  (let ((age (t/duration-between password-set-at (t/now))))
    (t/duration> age (t/duration :days 90))))
```

Step 3 — surface:

```scheme
(define (require-password-change) 'require-change)
(define (proceed-to-app) 'proceed)
(define user-t (list 'instant 1700000000))   ;; set long ago -> expired

(cond
  ((password-expired? user-t) (require-password-change))
  (else (proceed-to-app)))
```

The types keep the comparison honest. `t/duration>` compares durations to durations; it would refuse if you passed it an instant.

## Example 5 — Serializing a full record

Raw input: a fully-populated `order` in memory.

```scheme
(define (uuid s) (list 'uuid s))
(define (make-order . fields) (cons 'order fields))
(define order
  (make-order
    :id (uuid \"abc-123\")
    :placed-at (t/now)
    :total (money :amount 4086 :currency 'USD)
    :items (list (money :amount 4086 :currency 'USD))
    :status 'pending))
```

Serialize. Because an `order` is already a self-describing tagged list, writing and reading are structure-preserving.

```scheme
(define (slat/write v) v)
(define (slat/read v) v)

(slat/write order)
;; ⇒ (order :id (uuid \"abc-123\") :placed-at (instant 1720000000)
;;          :total (money :amount 4086 :currency USD)
;;          :items ((money :amount 4086 :currency USD)) :status pending)
```

Round-trip:

```scheme
(equal? order (slat/read (slat/write order)))
;; ⇒ #t
```

Bytes in, bytes out, same record. Every typed value survives.

## The general shape

For every value:

1. Validate at the door — raw → typed, with an error if bad.
2. Operate on typed values in the middle — arithmetic, comparisons, transformations.
3. Render for output only at the last moment — typed → string, formatted for the audience.

Three phases. Never mix them. The middle is where the program's logic lives; the ends are where the world lives; the discipline is what keeps them separate.")
